Body:
@prassanna-ravishankar's point about stable identity over display name extends further — agent identity doubles as the scoping mechanism for shared memory. When teams of agents need persistent knowledge (not just transient messages), the routing layer and the storage layer have to agree on who owns what.

The approach I landed on after iterating through several multi-agent architectures: each agent writes to its own namespace (scoped by a stable `agent_id`), and cross-agent recall is always explicit — Agent B pulls from Agent A's namespace only when it genuinely needs that context, not by default broadcast. This prevents knowledge contamination while keeping team-level sharing available. Importance-weighted decay handles staleness without manual TTLs.

If you're looking at reference implementations for the persistence layer, here's the setup I run for a 9-agent system: [`docker-compose.yml`](https://github.com/dakera-ai/dakera-deploy/blob/main/docker/docker-compose.yml) and the [environment variable docs](https://github.com/dakera-ai/dakera-deploy/blob/main/examples/environment-variables.md) showing agent isolation and decay config.
